**Critical path:** S5A's ETF contract/read-model/manifest interface freeze proceeds alongside independent S3/S4 work because it changes no v0.3.0 runtime. The pure Rust crate bootstrap is complete. Existing S5B/C/D1/D2 outputs are provisional candidate evidence against the S5A draft; they do not make the contract accepted. A full-width tiny-input counterexample showed that the prior Q64 quote materially underquoted exact invariant output; a working Q96 candidate now passes a reproducible corpus of 297 unique tiny-input cases over every valid two-asset weight step, plus the existing full-width and bounded-domain quote suites, with no overpayment and at most one-unit shortfall. The [Q96 interval proof](../rebalancing/evidence/q96-interval-proof.md) has passed independent source-level review; its private exponential helper now enforces the proved `0..=26Q96` argument domain, with the endpoint and first out-of-range unit covered. An isolated Localnet SBF probe measured 27,432–28,059 CU for the pure quote path; it excludes integrated account, oracle, CPI and scheduler costs. The new 29,106-vector three-asset model corpus covers every positive 100-bps weight partition and all ordered asset pairs, and demonstrates exact-curve-bounded quotes with strict same-mark post-fee USD drift improvement for its generated scenarios. The matrix also records a material caveat: 28,999 of 29,106 generated quotes reduce total marked NAV even though composition drift improves (maximum sampled runtime-quote loss 108 bps), before applying the still-unfrozen oracle-relative quote band. Drift improvement alone is therefore not an economic safety policy; S5D2/S5A must resolve fee and price-band protections against these scenarios. This closes only sampled-model drift evidence, not actual fee accounting, post-CPI drift or market execution. A separate 60-case selected near-`u64` three-asset corpus spans ten boundary/asymmetric/near-equal weight profiles and all ordered pairs with reserves above half of `u64::MAX`; all 60 samples also lose point-marked NAV while improving drift (maximum runtime quote loss 107 bps). This is finite custody/mark-boundary evidence, not exhaustive state or CPI proof. A 79,466-row Q96 high-precision interval corpus now corroborates sampled log/exp/power bounds against the reviewed analytical proof; its 20,054 exponential rows cover before/at/after every `z >> 8` range-reduction transition and the final two supported inputs. Decimal references and finite base/exponent samples are not full-domain certification. A dependency-free exact-rational ledger checker re-evaluates the proof numerics and selected source anchors; it is arithmetic corroboration, not a formal proof. S5B core-math evidence may be independently reviewed and accepted over documented parameterized domains without choosing production values; S5A finalizes production fee and oracle/risk policy after reviewing S5B, S5C and S5D2 evidence. Current steering (11 October 2026) permits modular S5E code and host/Localnet validation against explicit draft candidates, plus independent read-only S5F/S5G client/UI work; this supersedes the earlier code-start gate. Devnet deployment, pool initialization, seed, swaps and funded use remain fail-closed until the final accepted S5A contract/policy freeze and all required math, oracle/risk and fee/economics decisions are accepted. Integrated ETF target-runtime compute and actual SPL CPI custody/share reconciliation belong to S5E validation after the program exists, so they are not preconditions to start S5E. Review all S5B/C/D1/D2 findings into S5A amendments, then enable risk-increasing Devnet S5E actions only after the required math/oracle/fee decisions are accepted. Complete and review the S5F read-model/DTO contract before production client behavior; keep S5G presentation synthetic until it consumes that accepted contract and the Android build path is available. Real ETF Devnet integration waits for the current v0.3.0 gates, the separate ETF program gates, and a usable wallet/RPC/asset path. Then run version-specific security review → soak → separate future release decision. **Parallel product path:** card/import/scene identity → generation and deterministic rendering → save/share/remix → native integration → two-device proof. Sprint 10 orders may run in a separate isolated branch after their contract is frozen, but must not share unowned program entry points or app wiring with S5–S9. Detailed exclusive work sets, interfaces and gates are in the [checklist](onchain-portfolio-rebalancing-sprint-checklist.md). Governance signers and any future pilot cap remain out-of-sprint release decisions; no current sprint checkpoint tests Mainnet. Program-level safe failure and exit constraints remain technical invariants, not optional user choices.

### Human-in-the-loop sprint checkpoints

Each sprint ends at a human review checkpoint **only after the feature-specific interactive UI is ready**. Engineering owns code/build/program validation before handoff; the developer then follows UI steps against the explicitly named browser fixture, Localnet, Devnet or Testnet, records the exact commit and visible evidence, and accepts/rejects/blocks the gate. Unit tests and agent reports do not substitute for a UI walkthrough. Mainnet is never a developer checkpoint environment. The full UI prerequisites and click-by-click procedures are in the checklist's [Human developer checkpoints](onchain-portfolio-rebalancing-sprint-checklist.md#14-human-developer-checkpoints).

| Checkpoint | Developer verifies before proceeding |
