<file path="docs/rebalancing/evidence/s2b-auction-math.md">
# S2B auction math package evidence

**Status:** local pure-math package; not wired into an instruction and not a
financial activation approval.

## Frozen-contract reconciliation

The checklist's S2B row requests testing at the S0F maximum permitted
fee/bounty. The frozen sources do not define such a numeric maximum:

- `docs/rebalancing/spec-v1.md` says fee and bounty values remain unapproved
  until S4 economics and approval.
- `docs/rebalancing/contracts/accounts-instructions-v1.md` leaves fee values
  and bounty values unset for later S1/S2/S4 contract passes.
- The checklist S0F row describes an unfunded interface freeze and explicitly
  says numeric financial parameters remain unset.

This package therefore accepts explicit policy fee, bounty, and NAV-loss caps
as required arguments. It rejects missing caps and any supplied fee/bounty
outside them. Boundary tests use values passed by the test policy; they do not
claim those values are approved S0F maxima. S4 must publish measured numeric
parameters and an amended contract before the integrated instruction can be
activated for a funded flow.

## Math contract

- Amounts are raw SPL base units (`u64`); decimals are bounded at 18.
- Price input is a caller-validated positive `u64` in USD × 1e9 per whole
  token. This module does not fetch or validate oracle accounts.
- Token values use checked `u128` multiplication and floor division into the
  shared USD × 1e9 unit. Zero-valued trade legs are rejected as dust.
- The curve floors the incoming amount after the fee; NAV counts the gross
  incoming amount because the fee remains holder-owned, floor-rounded.
  Outgoing and bounty value round up in the NAV-loss guard, so token-decimal
  dust cannot make a fill appear cheaper. Portfolio-weight calculations use
  the same documented floor valuation on both sides of the transition.
- Dutch rate is an integer basis-point multiplier, interpolated from start to
  end with floor rounding. 10,000 means parity; a higher rate requires more
  input value per output value. The caller supplies the bounded curve values.
- `sell_amount` is in outgoing-token raw units and cannot exceed inventory or
  remaining size. A partial fill decrements remaining size by exactly that
  amount.
- The taker transfers gross incoming units; the fee is retained in the vault
  and is removed from the taker's curve credit using ceil rounding. The
  bounty is paid from the same incoming-asset reserve and subtracts from its
  post-fill balance. This convention is explicit for the pure model; S2D/S4
  must preserve it or revise the contract and vectors before wiring.
- Fill acceptance requires the exact post-fill drift ratio to be strictly
  lower, no crossing past target weight in the sold asset, and a NAV loss no
  greater than the passed cap. Fraction comparison uses Euclidean division
  rather than multiplication, avoiding cross-product overflow.

The accepted-vector property test enumerates integer partial sizes for
balanced-target two-asset portfolios and asserts every accepted fill strictly
improves drift and respects the zero-loss bound. Other vectors cover curve
endpoints/rounding, decimal scaling/dust, exact policy caps, overflow, loss,
inventory, remaining-size, price, and overshoot rejection.

## Limits / integration handoff

This module is not linked from `lib.rs`, intentionally: S2D owns auction
instruction wiring and the shared IDL. It has no token CPI, custody access,
oracle account parser, start/stop hysteresis, or fee-ledger state. The
caller-supplied fixed-point price is only safe after the S2A oracle adapter
validates feed ownership, identity, verification, freshness, confidence, and
depeg policy. Rounding granularity remains the documented fixed USD × 1e9
floor; deployment-scale values that exceed checked `u128` operations reject.

## Validation

- `rustfmt --edition 2021 .../auction_math.rs .../tests/auction_math/core.rs`:
  passed.
- Standalone `rustc --edition=2021 --test .../auction_math.rs` harness:
  11/11 tests passed. The module is intentionally not linked from `lib.rs` in
  this package, so the normal crate test target does not discover these tests
  yet; S2D owns the link-up and must rerun them through Cargo after integration.
- `cargo test -p coloyond-vault --lib`: existing integrated crate suite passed
  (16 tests); this does not include the unlinked S2B module.
- `cargo check --locked`: passed for the integrated crate; the isolated module
  was separately compiled by its standalone test harness.
- `cargo build-sbf --tools-version v1.57`: blocked before compilation because
  the installed SBF wrapper attempted to modify the protected
  `/Users/martin/.rustup/toolchains/solana` directory and received
  `Operation not permitted`. Also, because this package must not edit `lib.rs`,
  the current program build would not compile this module until S2D wires it.
</file>

<file path="docs/rebalancing/evidence/s2d-auction-state-machine.md">
# S2D auction state-machine evidence

**Status:** local Anchor implementation and generated IDL only. No admitted
policy account, initialized vault, deployed program, validator CPI proof, live
feed verification, funded taker, or executable market is claimed.

## Implemented interface

The program exposes `start_rebalance`, `fill_auction`, and `finish_or_expire`.
`RebalanceIntent` is a single PDA per vault, so a concurrent start cannot
create a second live auction. Its sequence is a per-fill compare-and-swap
counter; each accepted fill increments it atomically and uses the prior value
to derive a unique `BountyClaim` PDA. Replayed or stale fills fail before a
second state transition. `finish_or_expire` closes the intent PDA to its signed
caller on every terminal path, including stale-configuration recovery, so the
fixed PDA can be initialized again for a later auction and its rent is returned
to the caller that performed the close.

`fill_auction(expected_intent_sequence, sell_amount, gross_buy_amount,
bounty_amount)` preserves the S2C solver shape. The taker transfers the gross
incoming amount to the configured vault custody account and receives the
outgoing amount from the opposite custody account. Both CPIs, the bounded
math quote, accounting update, bounty liability, claim receipt, intent size,
and sequence update are in one Solana instruction. A CPI or later check error
therefore reverts the transaction state atomically. The fill has no immediate
bounty payout: it records a per-executor claim and aggregate Vault liability;
the separate payout path remains S4 work under S0F's `claim_bounty` contract.

## On-chain checks

- A versioned `AuctionPolicy` PDA is required and contains explicit oracle
  age/confidence/USDC deviation, drift, maximum trade value, cooldown, auction
  duration/curve, fee, bounty, per-fill and cumulative NAV-loss, and feed-map
  parameters. No numeric defaults are embedded. Start copies the relevant
  bounds into the intent. There is no direct policy initializer: the first
  executable policy must use S4C's governance-authorized proposal and delayed
  permissionless execution, while the vault is paused. The auction harness can
  execute SBF auction transfers against injected Localnet fixtures, but no
  browser-wallet CP2b walkthrough or bootstrap/seed rollback proof is recorded;
  the full bootstrap-to-policy sequence remains unvalidated.
- Start, fill, and price-dependent early finish require the two pinned Pyth
  `PriceUpdateV2` accounts. The S2A SDK adapter checks account address/owner,
  feed ID, `Full` verification, freshness, positive price, confidence and
  USDC parity under explicit policy inputs. Published feed account state is
  still unverified by S0B.
- Custody and mints are bound to `Vault`; SPL Token is the classic token
  program. The taker source/destination must have the expected signer owner
  and corresponding input/output mint. Direct donations are synchronized
  from live account balances; deficits reject.
- Start checks configured target drift, direction, cooldown, inventory, and a
  ceiling-rounded USD-Q9 maximum trade value. Each fill uses S2B's checked
  Dutch curve, size/inventory, no-overshoot, NAV-loss and post-fee drift
  improvement checks. It adds conservative per-fill NAV loss to a cumulative
  intent loss bound against start NAV; overflow rejects.
- A fresh unchanged push-feed timestamp may price multiple fills. Freshness is
  checked on every transition, timestamps cannot regress, and CAS sequence
  prevents transaction replay. Strictly increasing observations remain the
  separate S3 condition-module contract.
- Each booked bounty is capped by policy and by unencumbered seeded
  `bootstrap_reserve`; `bounty_liabilities` are subtracted from share backing.
  A stale target/policy revision makes fills reject but permits permissionless
  intent closure, avoiding a stranded PDA. Expiry and completed intents can
  close without current oracle data. In-kind redemption remains independent
  of oracle and auction accounts and risk pause.

## Validation and remaining gates

The package runs Anchor-native tests for policy bounds, bounty reserve
coverage, stale intent closure, sequence replay/overflow, unchanged fresh
feed reuse, and timestamp regression. S2A and S2B tests are linked into the
same crate suite. `idl/coloyond_vault.json` and the TypeScript IDL are generated
from the actual program entrypoints. The Solana v1.57 SBF build completed with
no stack-frame overflow diagnostics after boxing the auction accounts and
splitting fill math/CPI work into bounded helpers.

These are local compile/unit/SBF and IDL checks. The auction Localnet harness
uses genesis-injected vault, governance, policy and token fixtures; it does not
yet exercise the current `initialize_vault` path or prove its seed CPI rollback
and follow-on S4C policy delay. The safe source sequence now exists, but those
transactions remain unverified. S0B feed accounts/authority and S4 economics/
configuration remain admission blockers. Do not enable funded UI, Devnet
operation, or public custody based on this package.
</file>

