# S5 scheduler source audit

**Status:** TukTuk plus Helium's separate Cron program are the first replaceable L1 scheduling candidate. Read-only Devnet account checks now confirm both programs are executable and inspect the live TukTuk config, queue census, and a bounded sample of unrelated task payloads. The project has not created a queue or Cron job, registered an ETF task, confirmed any run transaction, or established public crank-turner availability. Program presence and unrelated recurring work are not ETF scheduler integration.

The upstream source review is pinned to the exact Helium `main` revision `77dabc07580496517b6c390446daf4962fb3c39a`; file hashes and the reviewed API surface are recorded in [the source-provenance manifest](helium-tuktuk-source-pin-20261011.yaml). This is source provenance only. It is not evidence that the Devnet ProgramData fingerprints below were built from this revision.

## Findings from official Helium sources

- TukTuk is a Solana L1 task queue/crank program. Maintainer instructions describe running a crank-turner operator; no guaranteed public Devnet executor was established.
- Helium also maintains a separate on-chain Cron program (`cronAjRZnJn3MTP3B9kE62NWDrjSuAPVXf9c4hu4grM`) that can schedule compiled instructions into a TukTuk queue. Its official TypeScript example creates a recurring cron job, adds a `CompiledV0` instruction and describes it running every minute. The official README says the Cron must remain funded to create queue tasks; insufficient funding can remove a scheduled task from the queue, after which the operator must call the documented `cron requeue` flow. Shutdown requires closing each cron-transaction account before closing the Cron job. This is a stronger recurring candidate than an ETF-authored child-task loop, but it still needs an external crank-turner, funded Cron account and queue, and an explicit monitor/stop/recovery policy. A schedule is time-based; the ETF `poke` instruction must still evaluate oracle freshness and eligibility on-chain.
- Queue creation documents a refundable 1 SOL minimum deposit, and each crank has a separate reward intended to cover transaction/priority fees. Queue capacity/rent and repeated task creation also have costs. A refundable minimum is not continuing automation funding.
- Queue authority and custom signer choices can expose queue funds; use a narrowly scoped queue and dedicated signer.
- Use compiled tasks only for the no-HTTP project architecture. TukTuk's `RemoteV0` obtains a signed transaction from an HTTP server and therefore requires a backend service.
- The source defines a 40-byte task-description limit and compiled instruction account indexes as `u8`. Exact ETF account list, task size/rent, transaction size, nested CPI behavior, compute units and crank reward remain to be probed; there is no single source-level limit that proves the composed ETF instruction fits.
- TukTuk `TriggerV0` itself still has only `Now` and `Timestamp(i64)` variants; neither is an interval nor account-condition trigger. Recurrence can instead be composed through the separate Cron program, which schedules compiled tasks into TukTuk. TukTuk's return-data/free-task path remains a possible alternative, not a requirement if Cron passes the exact ETF proof. Neither path is an oracle-trigger service: scheduled `poke` calls must evaluate conditions in the ETF program, and each actual run still requires an external crank-turner.
- The maintained TukTuk `Anchor.toml` declares both program IDs under `programs.localnet`, not `programs.devnet`; official examples document Devnet RPC usage, and the direct account queries below now confirm both programs are nevertheless executable on Devnet. Program presence does not prove binary/source correspondence, Cron scheduling behavior, ETF task execution or keeper availability.

## Devnet status

The read-only probe at [`tools/rebalancing/etf-scheduler-probe`](../../tools/rebalancing/etf-scheduler-probe/README.md) queried `https://api.devnet.solana.com`. The program account was confirmed executable under the expected loader, with space 36 and 1,001,141,440 lamports. A later account scan at confirmed slot `509634043`, block time `1791657675` (2026-10-10 18:41:15 UTC), found Devnet genesis `EtWTRABZaYq6iMfeYKouRu166VU2xqa1wcaWoxPkrZBG`, config PDA `HGBovqKte26DbEMBma3T1TvvdmAFYSSgjncRoKECqfXq`, `min_task_queue_id=2`, `next_task_queue_id=315`, and `min_deposit=1,000,000,000` lamports. The confirmed queue read was at slot `509634043` (not older than the config read); it found 312 `TaskQueueV0` accounts, 98 with task bitmap entries, 628 total occupied task IDs, 19 with zero queue-authority count, and configured crank rewards from 0 to 1,000,000,000 lamports. These are live account facts, not a public-executor or liveness claim. Queue-account lamports include rent-exempt allocation and are not reported as spendable funding. The `hpl-crons` queue was one example with occupied task IDs; no task payload or execution transaction was inspected. No scheduler transaction has been sent.

### TukTuk Cron source and current Devnet account check — 11 October 2026

The official [Helium TukTuk README](https://github.com/helium/tuktuk) documents cron jobs as a separate program that queues tasks on a schedule and says the cron must remain funded. Its [cron example](https://github.com/helium/tuktuk/blob/main/typescript-examples/src/cron-memo.ts) uses `@helium/cron-sdk` and `@helium/tuktuk-sdk`, stores a compiled instruction, and configures `0 * * * * *` as an every-minute schedule. The source declares the Cron program ID above and exposes queue-cron-task instructions. A read-only confirmed RPC query at Devnet genesis `EtWTRABZaYq6iMfeYKouRu166VU2xqa1wcaWoxPkrZBG` found TukTuk executable at slot `509706755` and Cron executable at slot `509706759`; both are owned by `BPFLoaderUpgradeab1e11111111111111111111111` with 36-byte program accounts. Their program-account lamports were 1,001,141,440 and 1,141,440 respectively; these are executable-account balances, not queue or scheduler operating funds. The later queue snapshot at slot `509706767` (block time `1791675068`, 2026-10-10 23:31:08 UTC / 2026-10-11 07:31:08 +0800) found 312 queues, 98 with occupied tasks and 677 occupied task IDs. The bounded unrelated-task sample had one task disappear during reads and reported it as a race. No ETF/Cron task was created and no transaction was sent. These reads prove point-in-time program presence, not source/binary match, recurring schedule execution, a public crank-turner, funding, or ETF compatibility.

### Current Devnet identity and scheduler refresh — 11 October 2026

The probe confirmed genesis `EtWTRABZaYq6iMfeYKouRu166VU2xqa1wcaWoxPkrZBG` at slot `509699679`; its TukTuk account remains executable under the upgradeable loader. The config read was at slot `509699683`, and the queue census at `509699688` again found 312 queues, 98 with occupied task bitmaps, 676 occupied task IDs, and crank rewards from 0 to 1,000,000,000 lamports. A bounded sample identified both `RemoteV0` and `CompiledV0` tasks; one task disappeared during the snapshot and was reported as a race. This confirms live account decoding on the pinned cluster only. It does not prove ETF task compatibility, execution, recurring liveness, queue spendable funds, or an available external crank-turner. No transaction was created or sent.

### Latest confirmed Devnet program and queue refresh — 11 October 2026

The read-only probe was rerun against the pinned Devnet genesis. TukTuk remained executable at slot `509744577` (ProgramData read `509744581`); Cron remained executable at `509744592` (ProgramData read `509744596`). Their deployed-byte SHA-256 fingerprints were unchanged: TukTuk `372aaf2b16a212a0fde3269574b3de18dd99178ff730cc2b609c45d9bf8a473a` and Cron `a2ee82589b7241bcdc167921e4121b7bd5e0aaebcd22c09101a525dfcf036fa1`. The config, queue census and block-time reads were at slots `509744604`, `509744609` and `509744609`; the block time was `1791684107` (`2026-10-11 02:01:47 UTC`). The census again found 312 queues, 98 with task bitmaps, 676 occupied task IDs and crank rewards from 0 to 1,000,000,000 lamports. A task disappeared between queue and task reads and was labeled a snapshot race. This confirms the deployed fingerprints had not changed since the prior capture; it still does not match them to the newly pinned source commit. No queue/task was created and no transaction was sent. Structured source and live-read values are in the [source-provenance manifest](helium-tuktuk-source-pin-20261011.yaml).

The read-only probe now makes a bounded sample of up to 15 live `TaskV0` reads from up to five queues and three occupied IDs per queue. It decodes the maintained Borsh fields, labels `CompiledV0` versus `RemoteV0` and `Now` versus timestamp triggers, validates compiled account indexes and signer-seed limits, checks task queue/ID against the sampled queue bitmap/capacity, derives each sampled task PDA from `[b"task", task_queue, task_id.to_le_bytes()]`, and fails when the RPC account key differs; it reports transaction payload bytes, rent field, crank reward and account lamports separately. It validates the currently pinned Devnet genesis before any live task-account read or Devnet label; malformed, Mainnet, and reset-era genesis values fail closed. Remote URLs and signer keys are omitted from output. Eight parser tests cover both source variants and trigger forms, index/seed failures, unknown variants, truncation, UTF-8/description and zero-padding behavior, bounded ID sampling, config fields, queue occupancy and base58 leading zeros. Nine offline size tests cover Borsh lengths, account-index bounds, boolean rejection, impossible account-class totals, PDA seed count/length, and the returned-task growth limit. Three PDA tests compare six standard-library derivations with `solana-pubkey` 2.4.0 vectors, validate the `u16` task-ID boundary and reject malformed queue keys. The returned-task model adds a strict direct `RunTaskReturnV0` Borsh parser and deterministic child-task checks for capacity, IDs, rewards, descriptions, compiled indexes, per-signer limits of 16 seeds/32 bytes each and the supported compiled-only policy; its 26 tests are offline fixtures, including maximum signer-seed boundaries, invalid free/occupied task-ID rejection including duplicate occupancy, task-queue address validation even for zero-child returns, supplied task-account PDA/order/emptiness checks, and CompiledV0 writable/readonly account-class count consistency. The offline Cron IDL surface adds six tests, bringing the combined probe suite to 59 passing tests, including the three ProgramData pointer/metadata/fingerprint tests. These models do not establish queue/task rent or reward funding, actual account ownership/PDA availability, transaction sizing, invocation, recurrence, or external crank-turner availability. No task was submitted or executed.

## Upgradeable-loader deployment fingerprints — 11 October 2026

The read-only probe was extended to follow each executable Program account's loader-encoded ProgramData pointer, require a same-or-newer confirmed RPC context, parse the loader's ProgramData slot/upgrade-authority metadata, and SHA-256 the remaining deployed byte region. At Devnet slot `509709553`, TukTuk's Program account pointed to `5eD2ns9eqYhRMQVaJo1cExfY1JQme6ybt2ZgjkXLA1cU`; its ProgramData read at slot `509709557` reports deployment slot `401569387`, upgrade authority `EwCiHKYRDrHfsaTv7S1Lf49yYbr62oFBaN3MVgzv9NU4`, 2,874,976 deployed-region bytes and SHA-256 `372aaf2b16a212a0fde3269574b3de18dd99178ff730cc2b609c45d9bf8a473a`. At program slot `509709568`, Cron pointed to `5f7TWpzsBFPKjaoAP5mZ5t2FZCV7ZuA9usZVr5K1PDTz`; its ProgramData read at slot `509709572` reports deployment slot `380941891`, the same upgrade authority, 722,176 deployed-region bytes and SHA-256 `a2ee82589b7241bcdc167921e4121b7bd5e0aaebcd22c09101a525dfcf036fa1`. The SDK-neutral standard-library decoder has tests for mutable and immutable authority metadata and malformed loader states. This pins two point-in-time on-chain byte regions for future comparison; it does **not** establish that either binary matches a particular source commit, that the source build is reproducible, or that an ETF task executes. The loader metadata layout follows Solana's [Upgradeable BPF loader source](https://github.com/solana-labs/solana/blob/master/sdk/program/src/bpf_loader_upgradeable.rs). No transaction was sent.

## Bounded live TaskV0 sample — 10 October 2026

The extended probe ran read-only against Devnet at queue-census slot `509646765`, block time `1791660732` (2026-10-10 19:32:12 UTC), on the expected genesis. It observed 312 queues and 632 occupied task IDs. The bounded sample selected five active queues and up to three bitmap IDs per queue; one task lookup in queue `crank-tester8` changed/disappeared between queue and task reads and was reported as a snapshot race rather than treated as a decode failure. Ten task accounts decoded successfully: three unrelated tasks were `RemoteV0`, seven were `CompiledV0`, and all sampled triggers were `Now`. The compiled task payloads ranged from 197 to 487 serialized bytes; remote task payloads occupied 137 or 197 bytes. Account lamports include rent/reward effects and are not evidence of queue spendable funds. No ETF payload was examined, no task was submitted, and no run transaction or external crank-turner was verified.

## Independent task-PDA verification — 11 October 2026

The read-only probe was refreshed at the same Devnet genesis. Its queue census was at confirmed slot `509660245` and observed 312 queues with 653 occupied task IDs. The bounded sample selected five queues: ten `TaskV0` accounts decoded successfully (seven `CompiledV0`, three `RemoteV0`), while one task disappeared between queue and task reads. For each successful account, the probe derived the expected PDA from TukTuk's [maintained queue instruction](https://github.com/helium/tuktuk/blob/main/solana-programs/programs/tuktuk/src/instructions/queue_task_v0.rs) seeds `[b"task", task_queue, id.to_le_bytes()]` and matched the RPC account address. The same standard-library derivation matches six offline vectors generated with `solana-pubkey` 2.4.0. This verifies only sampled TaskV0 address derivation and account identity; it does not prove ETF payload compatibility or execution. No queue/task was created, and no transaction or crank-turner was observed.

## Trigger and recurring-execution source audit — 11 October 2026

The maintained [`TriggerV0` enum](https://github.com/helium/tuktuk/blob/main/solana-programs/programs/tuktuk/src/state.rs) contains `Now` and `Timestamp(i64)` only; `Timestamp` becomes active once the current time reaches its timestamp. Neither variant repeats itself, and there is no state/account-change trigger in this enum. The maintained [`run_task_v0` implementation](https://github.com/helium/tuktuk/blob/main/solana-programs/programs/tuktuk/src/instructions/run_task_v0.rs) supports creating follow-up tasks from return data owned by the invoked program, and the current task declares `free_tasks` capacity. This is a possible source-level mechanism for recurring execution, not a proven ETF design: ETF `poke` would need a reviewed, bounded child-task protocol, exact returned payload and accounts, capacity/rent funding, stop/cancel behavior, and a distinct crank-turner that keeps submitting runs. The source also requires free task IDs and matching empty task PDAs, returned child rewards of `None` or the exact queue minimum, and per-task growth within Solana's 10,240-byte limit. The offline models now cover the direct return wire shape, a subset of deterministic child constraints and the account-growth formula, but they do not verify live ownership, PDA availability, rent/reward funding, queue solvency, stop policy or execution. A timestamp task can request one future execution but cannot itself express an unbounded interval. The separate Helium Cron program is now the leading recurring time-based candidate, while TukTuk's individual task triggers remain one-shot. Until an ETF Cron job (or reviewed ETF-authored requeue alternative) and external crank-turner pass the real invocation proof, recurring ETF execution remains unproven; neither scheduler creates an oracle-condition trigger or removes the crank-turner dependency.

### Pinned Cron implementation details — 11 October 2026

At the pinned source revision, `queue_cron_tasks_v0` is a compatibility handoff to `queue_cron_tasks_v1`; V1 validates the queue and the recorded schedule-task chain before advancing work. The legacy `requeue_cron_task_v0` handler returns `InstructionDeprecated`; the guarded recovery path is `requeue_cron_task_v1`, which requires the recorded prior task to be empty/default before queuing a replacement. The Cron source therefore has versioned account and instruction semantics that must be checked against any candidate Devnet binary before an adapter is frozen. The README's 1 SOL queue minimum is the observed config value: initialization transfers `TuktukConfigV0.min_deposit`, so the source does not make that amount a universal constant. See the immutable file links and SHA-256 values in the manifest. These findings do not change the candidate status or prove funded recurring execution.

### Cron client ABI and stop semantics — 11 October 2026

The pinned source's [`cron.json` IDL](https://github.com/helium/tuktuk/blob/77dabc07580496517b6c390446daf4962fb3c39a/tuktuk-program/idls/cron.json) is preserved at [`helium-cron-idl-0.3.0.json`](../../../tools/rebalancing/etf-scheduler-probe/fixtures/helium-cron-idl-0.3.0.json), SHA-256 `81958444d1719ce410e1007db279d8eace274387c5b803b5eaed5aa4b6026954`. The offline [`cron_idl.py`](../../../tools/rebalancing/etf-scheduler-probe/cron_idl.py) validator checks this exact IDL digest, program address, version, Anchor discriminators, ordered account metas and signer flags. Its tests also establish that Cron's remove/close instructions do not accept TukTuk task accounts. This fixture defines the reviewed construction surface; it does not prove the Devnet binary matches it.

The upstream `@helium/cron-sdk` wrapper has only initialization, lookup and PDA helpers plus `createCronJob` for `initialize_cron_job_v0`; add/remove/requeue/close are generated Anchor IDL methods or CLI operations. The source package declares version `0.1.1`, Anchor `^0.31.0` and `gitHead` `5a8bf0b7b88e5934ef8d774e686f7c95804fbb8d`, while the reviewed repository checkout is `77dabc07580496517b6c390446daf4962fb3c39a`. The published `@helium/cron-sdk`/IDL tarballs have not been compared with the pinned checkout, and the deployed ProgramData/source match remains unverified. Do not add the published wrapper as an exact pinned dependency until package provenance and this repository's Anchor compatibility are resolved.

Cron recurrence still requires separate crank submissions. Initialization schedules a coordinator task five minutes before the next occurrence. A crank runs that coordinator; Cron returns the next coordinator task and up to five scheduled action tasks; a crank must separately run each due action task. Cron therefore provides on-chain schedule state, not autonomous transaction submission or guaranteed liveness.

**Stop safety finding:** `remove_cron_transaction_v0` closes the Cron record and decrements a counter; `close_cron_job_v0` requires only that the counter is zero, then closes the job/name accounts and returns remaining return-account lamports. Neither instruction cancels TukTuk task accounts. The pinned [`queue_cron_tasks_v1`](https://github.com/helium/tuktuk/blob/77dabc07580496517b6c390446daf4962fb3c39a/solana-programs/programs/cron/src/instructions/queue_cron_tasks_v1.rs) copies each stored compiled payload into returned `TaskReturnV0` entries, which TukTuk materializes as independent `TaskV0` accounts. Closing Cron makes a still-pending coordinator return no new tasks, but an already materialized action task retains its compiled instruction and may still execute. Therefore record removal and job close are **not** an immediate stop guarantee.

The S5A contract and S5D1 adapter must require a two-layer stop: (1) atomically disable/increment an ETF scheduler generation, and make every scheduled `poke` carry and check its expected generation so surviving tasks become no-ops; then (2) read the TukTuk bitmap and dequeue every pending coordinator and action task using the queue authority before removing Cron transaction records and closing the job. TukTuk's pinned [`dequeue_task_v0`](https://github.com/helium/tuktuk/blob/77dabc07580496517b6c390446daf4962fb3c39a/solana-programs/programs/tuktuk/src/instructions/dequeue_task_v0.rs) requires the queue-authority signer/PDA and rejects a task whose existence bit is already clear because it may be mid-run. Treat that result as in-flight/unknown and reconcile it; do not claim cancellation. Read the resulting queue/job state back. A task that committed before the disable may have run; the disable must share the pool's write lock so later stale tasks cannot change ETF state. The actual queue-authority dequeue flow and this guard must be proven with a real scheduled task before CP5 or any operational stop claim.

### Direct returned-task wire and constraint model — 11 October 2026

The maintained [`RunTaskReturnV0` source](https://github.com/helium/tuktuk/blob/main/solana-programs/programs/tuktuk/src/instructions/run_task_v0.rs) defines direct return bytes as Borsh containing a vector of `TaskReturnV0` values followed by a vector of task-list account keys. Each returned task includes a one-shot trigger, compiled or remote transaction source, optional crank reward, `free_tasks` count and description. TukTuk reads the direct result strictly with `try_from_slice`, so malformed bytes and trailing bytes fail the run. It applies the 1,000-byte direct-return bound and offers an owned task-list account path for larger data. Returned tasks are constrained by the parent task's declared free-task capacity, caller-supplied unused IDs and matching empty task PDAs, queue capacity, the 40-byte description limit, a reward of `None` or exactly the queue minimum, per-task 10,240-byte growth, and available task/queue funds.

The new [`returned_tasks.py`](../../tools/rebalancing/etf-scheduler-probe/returned_tasks.py) parser checks only the direct Borsh structure and a subset of deterministic policy checks that can be evaluated from explicit fixture context. Its child validator now applies the same per-signer limit of 16 PDA seeds and 32 bytes per seed as the live-task decoder and offline size model, including independent validation of multiple signer groups. It parses `RemoteV0` but rejects it for the no-backend candidate, and it refuses the alternate `tasks_accounts` path rather than silently treating it as covered. A 166-byte, one-child, future-timestamp fixture exercises the candidate compiled-requeue shape, strict parser, direct-return size bound, and deterministic child constraints. Its keys and instruction bytes are placeholders; it uses the local Python Borsh helper, not Helium's Rust types, and is not the production ETF `poke` payload because the program ID/IDL/account privileges remain unfrozen. The parser does not verify return-data program ownership, account keys/owners, live queue bitmap state, rent or reward funding, free-task PDA existence, executor behavior, or repeated execution. Passing source-shaped offline vectors do not prove that an ETF program returns usable tasks or that TukTuk invokes them.

The separate offline [`payload_size.py`](../../tools/rebalancing/etf-scheduler-probe/payload_size.py) models Borsh `TaskV0` account data from parameterized compiled instruction shapes. It includes account-key vectors, instruction account/data vectors, signer-seed vectors, the one-shot trigger, description, enum tags and Anchor discriminator; it checks the referenced `u8` indices, the parser-matched maximum of 16 seeds per signer and 32 bytes per seed, and reports serialized account-data bytes. A sample one-key `Now` task with one one-account/two-byte instruction encodes to 168 serialized bytes. Five local size tests pass alongside the eight existing account-probe tests.

This serialized length is not the space TukTuk's queue instruction reserves. The maintained [`queue_task_v0`](https://github.com/helium/tuktuk/blob/main/solana-programs/programs/tuktuk/src/instructions/queue_task_v0.rs) allocates `8 + size_of::<TaskV0>() + transaction.size() + 4 + description.len() + 60`. Its [`CompiledTransactionV0::size()`](https://github.com/helium/tuktuk/blob/main/solana-programs/programs/tuktuk/src/state.rs) is a custom heuristic based on the transaction header, maximum referenced account index and compiled instructions; it does not include serialized signer-seed vectors. The host- and target-layout-dependent `size_of::<TaskV0>()` also is not the Borsh body length. Consequently the 168-byte sample must not be used to predict account allocation, rent or successful task creation. Exact allocation/serialization fit remains an S5D1 proof obligation once the ETF account list and target payload are frozen. Neither measurement predicts full transaction packet size, compute units, CPI feasibility, or crank-turner behavior; no actual ETF payload is sized.

### Cron funding model and source-build provenance — 11 October 2026

The pinned [`effective_tasks_per_queue_call`](https://github.com/helium/tuktuk/blob/77dabc07580496517b6c390446daf4962fb3c39a/solana-programs/programs/cron/src/schedule.rs#L25-L35) clamps the configured action cap to five. [`queue_cron_tasks_v1`](https://github.com/helium/tuktuk/blob/77dabc07580496517b6c390446daf4962fb3c39a/solana-programs/programs/cron/src/instructions/queue_cron_tasks_v1.rs#L203-L265) emits one successor coordinator plus one action task per nonempty selected Cron transaction record, so a fully populated pass emits at most six tasks; each emitted task requests the queue minimum crank reward. The Cron PDA transfers `emitted_task_count * min_crank_reward` to the task queue and pays any rent deficit for the used return-data PDAs while preserving its own rent minimum, as implemented in [`write_return_tasks`](https://github.com/helium/tuktuk/blob/77dabc07580496517b6c390446daf4962fb3c39a/tuktuk-program/src/write_return_tasks.rs#L127-L157). The minimum pre-pass balance modeled by [`cron_funding.py`](../../../tools/rebalancing/etf-scheduler-probe/cron_funding.py) is therefore:

`Cron rent minimum + used return-PDA rent deficits + emitted task count × queue minimum crank reward`.

The new calculator takes every rent, balance, cap, outstanding/empty record count and reward as explicit input. It does not set Devnet defaults, estimate initial setup or queue deposit, include crankers' transaction fees, validate the serialized task accounts, or imply executor availability. It also models source ordering on underfunding: previously paid return-account rent deficits can remain paid, but the crank reward is transferred only after all rent writes and the combined balance check succeed. Its tests pin coordinator-only, empty-record, reset/re-arm, five-action/six-task, exact-threshold, rent-deficit, partial-top-up, no-reward-on-stand-down and overflow behavior. These are offline source-model checks; actual account balances/rent, task sizing and queue solvency remain unobserved.

The pinned upstream repository's [CI workflow](https://github.com/helium/tuktuk/blob/77dabc07580496517b6c390446daf4962fb3c39a/.github/workflows/solana-programs.yaml) and [build action](https://github.com/helium/tuktuk/blob/77dabc07580496517b6c390446daf4962fb3c39a/.github/actions/build-anchor/action.yaml) recipe is now recorded in the source manifest: Ubuntu, Rust 1.82.0, Solana CLI 2.1.6, Anchor 0.31.1 and Node 18.12.1; the workflow invokes the pinned Anchor action with `TESTING=true`. This host has Solana 2.3.0 / platform-tools v1.48, SBF Rust 1.84.1 and Anchor 0.32.1, so its toolchain does not reproduce that CI matrix. A Cron comparison build with the cached v1.57 SBF platform tools was attempted from the pinned source but stopped before compilation: Cargo could not fetch the pinned `tuktuk-program` Git revision because GitHub DNS was unavailable. No clean pinned-toolchain build or byte-for-byte comparison with the recorded Devnet ProgramData hashes has been performed. `source_binary_match` remains unverified; no deployment, gate, or adapter compatibility is inferred.

## Required proof before S5/CP5 can pass

1. [x] Read Devnet genesis and prove the exact TukTuk program account exists, is executable, and has the expected loader owner (one point-in-time read; refresh before any task creation).
2. Wallet-sign queue creation/funding; record the 1 SOL deposit separately from queue/task rent and per-crank reward.
3. Register a compiled task with the exact public ETF `poke` instruction; record registration signature, queue/task PDAs, task ID, trigger and decoded payload, then read back the task account independently.
4. Capture a separate confirmed task-run transaction signed by the identified external crank-turner. Its inner instruction must invoke exactly `poke` and its before/after pool state must show eligibility/maintenance only.
5. If recurring behavior is required, first freeze a protocol-native one-shot/requeue contract, then prove every returned task, account, rent/reward funding, stop condition and successive external crank transaction. A single `Now`/`Timestamp` task is not recurring evidence.
6. Separately prove a trader wallet supplies SPL input for `swap_exact_in`. The scheduler invocation must not be described as a trade.
7. Prove permissionless direct `poke`, swaps and in-kind exits still work if the scheduler is unavailable.

If the Helium Cron/TukTuk path fails the direct Devnet checks, evaluate a demonstrated L1 scheduler against the same `poke` contract. Hydra source defines a base-layer program (`Hydra17i1feui9deaxu6d1TzSQMRNHeBRkDR1Awy7zea`) distinct from its Ephemeral Rollup program (`eHyd5BU8QffvHi4GnXwxrK4WpS7pM2x9UGKHBWii7mf`); Devnet deployment and source/release correspondence are unverified, so read-only `getAccountInfo` plus loader/ProgramData hash comparison and `poke` compatibility checks remain before consideration. MagicBlock Ephemeral Rollups change the execution environment and are not a drop-in L1 replacement. If no candidate passes, CP5 and scheduler-automation claims stay blocked; independent S5E work may proceed once its S5A/math/oracle/fee gates pass. A local simulation is not a substitute for CP5.

